{% extends 'admin/_main/layout.html.twig' %}
{% block title %}Tracking Policies | Admin{% endblock %}
{% block admin_body %}
    {# A list screen and nothing else.

       It used to carry the create form above the grid and edit by reloading itself with
       `?edit={id}`. `app.css:7764` clamps a `.content-frame` holding a top-level `.table-card` to
       `calc(100vh - 3rem)` — the model that gives every grid in this app its sticky header and one
       scroll region — and that clamp assumes the grid IS the page. With a 693px form inside the
       clamped frame ahead of it the card was left 44px of 852, the scroll region's 220px floor
       overflowed a card that is `overflow: hidden`, and not one data row reached the screen. The
       policies were all in the HTML and none of them was visible.

       So there is no form on this page. Create is `admin_tracking_policy_new`, edit is
       `admin_tracking_policy_edit`, and the grid gets the whole frame. #}
    <section class="panel compact-panel">
        <div class="panel-title-row">
            <div>
                <p class="eyebrow">Products</p>
                <h1>Tracking Policies</h1>
            </div>
            <div class="panel-actions">
                <a class="button back-button" href="{{ path('admin_product_detail_index') }}">Back</a>
                <a class="button primary" href="{{ path('admin_tracking_policy_new') }}">Add a policy</a>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    {% for msg in app.flashes('success') %}<div class="flash flash-success">{{ msg }}</div>{% endfor %}
    {% for msg in app.flashes('error') %}<div class="flash flash-error">{{ msg }}</div>{% endfor %}

    {% include 'admin/_row_actions_no_js.html.twig' %}

    {# No filter row and no search box, deliberately.

       A tracking policy is a named rule, not a record: the whole argument for the table is that a
       change is made in one place instead of on twenty thousand products, so an installation holds a
       handful of rows and they all fit on the screen at once. A per-column filter row would add
       eight cells of chrome above a table nobody has to look for anything in, and a mode filter over
       three values on five rows narrows nothing. The one question this grid genuinely cannot answer
       by eye — WHICH products are on a policy — is not a filter over this table but a jump to the
       product list, and the product list has no tracking-policy filter to jump into. When it gets
       one, the Products count here becomes the link to it. #}
    <section class="table-card no-paginate">
        <div class="table-header">
            <h2>Policies</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="table-scroll-region">
            <div class="wide-table-wrap">
                <table class="admin-grid-table">
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>Policy</th>
                            <th>Identity</th>
                            <th>In</th>
                            <th>Out</th>
                            <th>Expiry</th>
                            <th>Sentinels</th>
                            <th>Products</th>
                            <th>Actions</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        {% include 'admin/tracking_policy/_rows.html.twig' %}
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
        </div>
        <div class="table-footer">
            {% include 'admin/_table_count.html.twig' with {
                count: rows|length,
                total: rows|length,
                currentPage: 1,
                perPage: rows|length,
                label: 'policies'
            } only %}
        </div>
    </section>
{% endblock %}
